    <p class="paraCommon paraHeader"  >    <b>Green Education - No New Degree Required… </b>
      <span style="font-size:18px;display:block;margin-top:10px;"> Learning for green jobs is simpler than you think.</span>    
   </p>
        
    <p class="paraCommon"><b>You don’t need a “green degree” to do green work.</b></p>

    <p class="paraCommon">Let’s clear the confusion first. </p>

    <p class="paraCommon">Green jobs don’t sit inside one special college, one fancy course, or one new education stream. There is no secret “Green University” that people forgot to tell you about.</p>
    <p class="paraCommon">Green work happens across many normal subjects that already exist like <b>Agriculture, Food, Environment, Climate, Forests, Energy, Waste, Water, Design, Data, Logistics, Communication…</b> you get the idea.</p>
    <p class="paraCommon">People doing green jobs today come from <b>science, commerce, arts, ITI, engineering, design, humanities and sometimes from totally unrelated backgrounds.</b></p>
    <p class="paraCommon">What matters is not <i>what degree you have,</i> but:</p>
    <p class="paraCommon">
        – what you’re curious about <br />
        – what skills you’re willing to build<br />
        – whether you want your work to actually mean something<br />
    </p>
    <p class="paraCommon">Good news?</p>
    <p class="paraCommon">You don’t need years to figure this out.</p>
    <p class="paraCommon">With <b>70–100 hours of focused self-learning,</b> most people can become green-job ready. </p>
    <p class="paraCommon">No gatekeepers. </p>
    <p class="paraCommon">No pressure. </p>
    <p class="paraCommon">No “sir please certify me”.</p>
    <p class="paraCommon">“Relax. You’re not late. You’re not behind. You just need direction.”</p>
    <p class="paraCommon">Below are four simple ideas that explain how green education actually works in the real world.</p>
